{
 "slug": "possum",
 "title": "possum",
 "deck": "<em>possum, potes, potest</em>: \"I can\", \"I am able\", with an infinitive. And <em>debeo</em>, \"I must\", which works in the same way.",
 "desc": "GCSE Latin grammar, Chapter 3: possum in the present and imperfect with an infinitive, and debeo meaning must, with sentences to translate and forms to write.",
 "key": [
  [
   "<em>possum</em>",
   "I can, I am able"
  ],
  [
   "Present",
   "<em>possum, potes, potest, possumus, potestis, possunt</em>"
  ],
  [
   "Imperfect",
   "<em>poteram</em>: I could, I was able"
  ],
  [
   "With an infinitive",
   "<em>currere possum</em>: I can run"
  ],
  [
   "<em>debeo</em> + infinitive",
   "I must, I ought to"
  ]
 ],
 "marks": {
  "c": "possum or debeo",
  "v": "the infinitive"
 },
 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "What possum is",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "<em>possum</em> means \"I can\" or \"I am able\". It is <em>sum</em> with <em>pot-</em> in front. Like \"can\" in English, it needs another verb to finish it: an infinitive."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servus <u class=\"v\">currere</u> non <u class=\"c\">potest</u>.",
      "en": "The slave <b>cannot run</b>.",
      "note": "<em>potest</em> is \"he can\" and <em>currere</em> is \"to run\". After \"can\", English drops the \"to\"."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "Where <em>sum</em> begins with <em>s</em>, <em>possum</em> has <em>pos-</em>: <em>possum, possumus, possunt</em>.",
      "Where <em>sum</em> begins with <em>e</em>, <em>possum</em> has <em>pot-</em>: <em>potes, potest, potestis</em>.",
      "The infinitive usually comes just before it."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "forms",
   "h2": "The forms",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Person",
       "Present",
       "English",
       "Imperfect",
       "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "I",
        "<b>pos</b>sum",
        "I can",
        "<b>pot</b>eram",
        "I could, I was able"
       ],
       [
        "you (one)",
        "<b>pot</b>es",
        "you can",
        "<b>pot</b>eras",
        "you could"
       ],
       [
        "he, she, it",
        "<b>pot</b>est",
        "he can",
        "<b>pot</b>erat",
        "he could"
       ],
       [
        "we",
        "<b>pos</b>sumus",
        "we can",
        "<b>pot</b>eramus",
        "we could"
       ],
       [
        "you (more than one)",
        "<b>pot</b>estis",
        "you can",
        "<b>pot</b>eratis",
        "you could"
       ],
       [
        "they",
        "<b>pos</b>sunt",
        "they can",
        "<b>pot</b>erant",
        "they could"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "note": "The imperfect is <em>pot-</em> with the imperfect of <em>sum</em>: <em>poteram, poterat</em>. Translate it \"could\" or \"was able to\". The infinitive is <em>posse</em>, \"to be able\"."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "debeo",
   "h2": "<em>debeo</em> with an infinitive",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "<em>debeo</em> means \"I owe\". With an infinitive it means \"I must\", \"I ought to\" or \"I should\". It is a regular 2nd conjugation verb: <em>debes, debet, debent</em>."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servi <u class=\"v\">laborare</u> <u class=\"c\">debent</u>.",
      "en": "The slaves <b>must work</b>."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "nautae <u class=\"v\">discedere</u> <u class=\"c\">debebant</u>.",
      "en": "The sailors <b>had to leave</b>.",
      "note": "<em>debebant</em> is imperfect: \"had to\" is the clearest English."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Translating them",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Find <em>possum</em> or <em>debeo</em>, then the infinitive that goes with it.",
      "With <em>possum</em>, drop the \"to\": \"he can run\". Or keep it with \"able\": \"he is able to run\".",
      "<em>non</em> with <em>possum</em> is \"cannot\" or \"was not able to\".",
      "With <em>debeo</em>: \"must\", \"ought to\", \"should\", or \"had to\" in the imperfect."
     ]
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "puella <u class=\"v\">dormire</u> non <u class=\"c\">poterat</u>.",
      "en": "The girl <b>could not sleep</b>."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "<em>potest</em> is \"he can\"; <em>possunt</em> is \"they can\". Check the ending as you would with <em>sum</em>.",
      "<em>poterat</em> is past: \"he could\", \"he was able\".",
      "Keep the infinitive with its verb: <em>currere potest</em>, \"he can run\".",
      "<em>debeo</em> on its own is \"I owe\". With an infinitive it is \"I must\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
